This directory contains files with statistics that can be output from CLUBB.

Named stats selections (opt-in):

stats.json is the hierarchical catalog. A category selects all the stats below
it. Short names include every matching category, including both standard and
non-standard branches; slash-separated paths select just one subtree.
An individual variable is selected with var:NAME. Commas and '+' combine
categories and variables, with duplicate stats emitted only once.

Examples, from the repository root:

  python run_scripts/run_scm.py bomex -stats core,radiation,silhs
  python run_scripts/run_scm.py bomex -stats core+wp2_budgets
  python run_scripts/run_scm.py bomex -stats standard/radiation
  python run_scripts/run_scm.py bomex -stats var:rtm,var:wp2
  python run_scripts/run_scm.py bomex -stats standard
  python run_scripts/run_scm.py bomex -stats all
  python run_scripts/run_scm.py bomex -stats none
  python run_scripts/run_scm.py bomex -stats input/stats/fire_stats.in

'radiation' selects 32 entries (10 standard and 22 BUGSrad); 'standard/radiation'
selects only the 10 standard entries. An optional 'all/' prefix is accepted for
paths. Category names
match the JSON keys exactly, including case in names like Nrm_budgets.
Selections do not implicitly add core or any other category.

Existing namelist filenames still work and take precedence over category names.
Omitting -stats still uses standard_stats.in; explicitly selecting 'standard'
uses the revised JSON standard set. Existing stats files and test configurations
have not been migrated. Scalar, hydrometeor and SILHS category templates retain
their usual runtime expansion. Full SILHS subcolumn dumps are outside this
registry catalog and are not enabled by these selections.

To review the namelist without running CLUBB:

  python utilities/create_case_namelist.py bomex -stats core,radiation -output_dir /tmp/stats_review
  python utilities/stats_json_to_namelist.py standard/radiation
  python utilities/stats_json_to_namelist.py core+kk -output_file /tmp/core_kk_stats.in
  python utilities/stats_json_to_namelist.py -list

The helper emits only &clubb_stats_nl (or nothing for 'none'). The case generator
calls it directly and also disables stats output when -stats none is selected.
No intermediate stats file is needed. The helper accepts -json_file FILE to inspect
another catalog; model runs use this directory's stats.json.

Dash Run tab:

All, Standard and Core select the named JSON categories. Custom opens the
checkbox tree with all branches initially collapsed. Expand categories to
choose individual variables. Each single-line row shows name, units, grid
(thermo, momentum or surface), and long description, with horizontal scrolling
for long rows. Checkbox changes and previews run locally in the browser;
category disclosures open and close without a server callback.
Parent checkboxes select or clear all descendants and show a mixed state when
only some descendants are selected. Cancel leaves the
current run selection unchanged. Clear all followed by Use selection disables
statistics. Reload the dashboard after editing the catalog hierarchy or
changing a saved/legacy list outside the app.

Saved & legacy lists keeps the existing .in registries available separately.
Save stats config writes a new snapshot to input/stats/custom/NAME.in, which
appears immediately in that list. Names use letters, numbers, underscores and
hyphens; existing lists are never overwritten. These snapshots retain their
exact definitions when the main catalog changes. The directory is created on
first save and ignored by Git.
Reuse one from the CLI with -stats input/stats/custom/NAME.in. Opening a saved
or legacy list restores its exact variables in the tree, including partial
categories. If a list contains names outside the catalog, the editor warns
that applying the tree will omit those names.

To save a category set from the CLI:

  python utilities/stats_json_to_namelist.py core+standard/radiation -output_file input/stats/custom/core_radiation.in

Important notes:

Some variables are only non-zero when code options are enabled.  For example, 
the hydrometeors rrm, rim, etc. will only be non-zero when a microphysics
scheme is enabled.  Many radiation variables (e.g. upwelling short-wave 
radiative flux) will only be output if BUGSrad is being used.

Outputting matrix condition numbers can change your results.  This is because
the subroutines used to obtain those numbers (the LAPACK expert subroutines) 
perform an additional iterative refinement step after computing the solution
that isn't done by the standard subroutines.  Note that these variables also
greatly increase the computational expense of a simulation.  The matrix 
condition number variables are:
'wp23_matrix_condt_num', 'rtm_matrix_condt_num', 'thlm_matrix_condt_num', 
'rtp2_matrix_condt_num', 'thlp2_matrix_condt_num', 'rtpthlp_matrix_condt_num',
'up2_vp2_matrix_condt_num',  and 'windm_matrix_condt_num'.

Some Fortran compilers don't deal well with comments in namelists.  Avoid 
commenting out variable names within the stats namelist when possible.
